# Schema-First Development Rules

## Schema Guidelines
- YAML is the primary format for schema definitions, JSON is the canonical format for validation
- All schemas must follow OpenAPI 3.1 standards
- Base node schema includes: id, type, parent_ids, child_ids, metadata
- Use composition (JSON Schema `allOf`) for extending schemas
- Keep schemas focused on data structure, not behavior
- Include appropriate validation constraints in schemas
- Use consistent property naming across all schemas
- Version schemas explicitly and document changes

## Code Generation
- Never modify auto-generated code directly
- Regenerate types on schema changes
- Create wrapper or adapter classes for customization

## Testing Requirements
- Write tests before implementing features
- Include tests for failure cases and edge conditions
- Document any test coverage gaps

## Documentation
- Update documentation immediately when code or decisions change
- Document architecture decisions with context and alternatives
- Keep documentation concise and interconnected
- Use consistent terminology across all documentation
- Remove or update outdated information immediately

## Development Process
- Follow domain-driven design principles
- Prefer object-oriented over functional programming when appropriate
- Use composition over inheritance
- Document tech debt clearly with rationale
- Keep files under 400 lines where possible
- Create self-documenting code with clear naming

## Knowledge Graph Structure
- All entities form node-based directed graph structure
- Use consistent relationship types across the graph
- Design with both human readability and machine processing in mind
